  "name": "Carcino Embryonic Antigen-CEA",
  "code": "CEA",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<table><thead><tr><th><div>CEA Level (ng/mL)</div></th><th><div>Interpretation</div></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><div>Normal Levels</div></td><td><div>Normal CEA levels in the blood are typically less than 3 to 5 ng/mL, depending on the laboratory's reference range. Normal CEA levels are usually seen in healthy individuals with no evidence of cancer or other conditions that could cause CEA elevation.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Mildly Elevated Levels</div></td><td><div>Mildly elevated CEA levels (between 5 and 10 ng/mL) may be seen in certain non-cancerous conditions, such as inflammatory bowel disease (IBD), pancreatitis, or chronic smokers. Additional evaluation may be needed to determine the cause of the elevation.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Moderately Elevated Levels</div></td><td><div>CEA levels moderately elevated (between 10 and 50 ng/mL) may indicate the presence of certain cancers, including colorectal cancer, gastric cancer, or lung cancer. However, other non-cancerous conditions can also cause moderately elevated CEA levels. Further testing, including imaging and other diagnostic procedures, may be necessary to confirm the diagnosis.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Markedly Elevated Levels</div></td><td><div>Markedly elevated CEA levels (above 50 ng/mL) are more strongly associated with cancer, especially advanced or metastatic disease. However, other factors can also cause very high CEA levels. Comprehensive evaluation and consultation with an oncologist are essential for a definitive diagnosis and treatment plan.</div></td></tr></tbody></table>",
